     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §364-j, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5774\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to excluding\noutpatient blood clotting factor products and related services and\ntreatments from coverage by managed care provided to medical\nassistance recipients\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo maintain the provision of blood clotting factor and related\ntreatment and services furnished in connection with the care of\nhemophilia and other blood clotting disorders to Medicaid\nbeneficiaries outside of Medicaid managed care.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends section 364-j of the Social Services Law to maintain\nthe current exclusion of outpatient blood clotting factor products and\ntreatments and services furnished to care for persons with hemophilia\nfrom the Medicaid managed care program\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that the legislation would take effect\nimmediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nHemophilia and other bleeding disorders are conditions in which the\nblood does not clot properly due to a defective or missing protein.\nBlood clotting factor replacement therapy prevents and controls\nbleeding by temporarily replacing the missing or defective protein.\nThe administration, handling and dispensing of the product is a\ncomplex exercise, which requires the expertise of Hemophilia Treatment\nCenters and other expert providers, specialized pharmacy and unique\nstorage and refrigeration requirements.\n\nBecause of the relatively small (approximately 350) Medicaid\nbeneficiaries with hemophilia, the complex nature of the management of\nthe disease, and the expense associated with blood clotting factor\ntherapies, the Department of Health has not included clotting factor\nwithin the Medicaid managed care benefit package but has paid for it\ndirectly. Although the Department has previously considered \"carving\nin\" blood clotting factor and associated services into the benefit\npackage, it has repeatedly been determined that access to the\ncomprehensive care and therapies required by persons with these\ndisorders warranted keeping clotting factor outside of the Medicaid\nmanaged care program.\n\nThe Department has now proposed to carve in clotting factor and\nrelated services into the Medicaid managed care program, effective\nJuly 1, 2017. Consumers, advocacy organizations, Hemophilia Treatment\nCenters, the New York Blood Center (which procures, prepares and\ndelivers the clotting factor) and Medicaid managed care plans support\nthe continued \"carve-out\" of clotting factor and related services from\nthe Medicaid managed care program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone. The Department of Health has repeatedly indicated that carving\nin this benefit would not result in a savings to the Medicaid program.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
