 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Excludes outpatient blood clotting factor products, and other related services and treatments from managed care coverage for medical assistance recipients.",
 "title": "Excludes outpatient blood clotting factor products and other related services and treatments from managed care coverage for medical assistance recipients",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S5774",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S5774-2017",
     "printNo": "S5774",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 156\n\nTO THE SENATE:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nSenate Bill Number 5774, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the social services law, in relation to excluding\n      outpatient blood clotting factor products and related services and\n      treatments from coverage  by  managed  care  provided  to  medical\n      assistance recipients\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This  bill  would amend the Social Services Law to permanently exclude\nall outpatient blood clotting factor products and other  treatments  and\nservices  for  care of hemophilia and other blood clotting protein defi-\nciencies from Medicaid managed care. Such benefits are scheduled  to  be\nincluded,  or  \"carved-in\", to the State's Medicaid managed care program\non July 1, 2017.\n\n  In 2011, I established the Medicaid Redesign Team (MRT) to restructure\nthe State's Medicaid program, improve health outcomes, and  lower  Medi-\ncaid spending. A team of experts made several recommendations, including\na \"Care Management for All\" initiative to enroll all Medicaid recipients\nin  the  State's  Medicaid  managed  care  program.  This initiative has\nsuccessfully reduced costs by improving care coordination and  redirect-\ning fee-for-service Medicaid spending to Medicaid managed care organiza-\ntions.\n\n  As  drafted, however, this bill would delay the full implementation of\nthis important initiative. Individuals that suffer from  hemophilia  and\nother  blood  clotting  protein  deficiencies  are  already enrolled and\nreceive the majority of their services through  Medicaid  managed  care.\nYet this bill would reverse course, and permanently exclude all services\nand benefits related to these conditions from managed care.\n\n  Transitioning  all services back to a fee-for-service environment will\nhave significant consequences to the full  implementation  of  the  Care\nManagement  for  All  initiative,  including  increased  Medicaid costs.\nFurther, it will necessarily exclude a certain population of individuals\nfrom enrolling in Medicaid managed care plans, which will lead to  inef-\nficiencies  in the statewide delivery of health care services. For these\nreasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  However, I recognize the unique needs of  this  population  and  I  am\ntherefore expressly directing the Department of Health (DOH) to take the\nfollowing  steps.  First,  managed  care health plans will avoid service\ndisruptions by reporting to DOH on the progress of finalizing  contracts\nwith  each  individual's  provider  of  blood  clotting factor products.\nSecond, DOH will continue to discuss the transition with  each  individ-\nual, including offering a toll-free helpline for assistance.  Third, DOH\nwill coordinate with managed care plans and providers during the transi-\ntion to resolve any financial or billing-related issues.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
